Mild contact irritation on intact skin from undiluted topical essential oil application
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Localized burning post-application, Well-circumscribed erythema, Rapid improvement with washing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-015
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of intense burning and rapidly evolving skin erythema requiring assessment to exclude chemical burn or early cellulitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified sudden, intense localized burning and a spreading red rash following topical application. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate the depth of the cutaneous injury, definitively rule out a full-thickness chemical burn, and ensure the offending agent was safely decontaminated prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My skin is burning intensely right where I rubbed some undiluted peppermint oil an hour ago.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a localized burning and stinging sensation occurring shortly after topical essential oil application. Reports a well-circumscribed erythematous macule matching the exact application area. Notes the skin feels dry and slightly roughened. Pruritus is developing as the acute burning subsides. Complete absence of vesicular or bullous lesions. Rapid subjective improvement was noted upon initial washing of the affected area with water.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Widespread use of undiluted alternative topical remedies without understanding appropriate dilution protocols.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/78 | HR: 84 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, distressed by the burning sensation but hemodynamically stable.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals a well-circumscribed erythematous macule matching the application area precisely. The skin texture is dry and slightly roughened, with an intact epidermal barrier. No blistering, sloughing, or ulceration. Diagnostic triad confirmed: localized burning post-application, well-circumscribed erythema, and rapid improvement with washing. Sparing of adjacent skin not exposed to the oil. Normal core body temperature. No systemic signs of anaphylaxis.
Environmental Observations: Multiple small bottles of concentrated essential oils on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No known allergies; heavy use of unregulated topical supplements.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild contact irritation on intact skin from undiluted topical essential oil application - Status: Improving
Differentials:

Deep Chemical Burn - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact epidermal barrier, absence of blistering, and rapid clinical improvement following simple decontamination.

Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Immediate onset following chemical application, exact geographic demarcation, and absence of deep tissue induration or warmth.

Systemic Allergic Reaction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of airway compromise, normal hemodynamics, and rash restricted exclusively to the contact site.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The acute onset of localized burning and erythema following chemical exposure warranted urgent clinical assessment to accurately determine the depth of tissue damage. Delaying the evaluation could result in inadequate decontamination or misclassification of a deep chemical burn. The face-to-face home visit confirmed that the injury was restricted to superficial epidermal irritation, effectively avoiding unnecessary ED referral and facilitating immediate, targeted topical treatment and education.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Thorough secondary cleansing of the area with mild soap and copious water to remove any residual lipophilic oil. Application of a bland, unscented emollient to soothe the irritated epidermis.
Positive Behavioral Change: Pre-mix all essential oils with a carrier oil (like coconut or olive oil) immediately upon purchase to ensure safe dilution ratios are hardwired into future use, preventing repeated acute exposures.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only if blistering occurs or if the rash expands.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience sudden difficulty breathing, swelling of the face or tongue, or if the skin starts to form large fluid-filled blisters.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is superficial irritant contact dermatitis; individual sensitivities to specific oils can escalate with repeated exposures.
Clinical Course: Erythema and burning should fully resolve within 24 to 48 hours with conservative management and avoidance of the concentrated agent.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the red area develops painful blisters, if the skin begins to peel off entirely, or if pus develops.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Essential oils are highly concentrated plant chemicals. Applying them directly strips the protective barrier of the skin, causing a chemical irritation similar to a mild sunburn.
Actionable Steps: Never apply undiluted oils directly to the skin. Wash the area gently with soap and cool water. Apply a thick moisturizer twice daily until healed.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Essential oil skin irritation. Superficial redness only. Wash area well with soap. Apply basic moisturizer. Watch for large blisters or swelling.

SMS EDUCATION

Always mix essential oils with a carrier oil (like olive oil) before putting them on your skin. Wash the area immediately if it burns.

